  "result": "The gate hasn't checked the concept yet: both runs I made came back `SCOPE_VIOLATION`, and the cause is in the environment, not the two files. Every gate run makes the tool runtime save the command's output to a new file under `.runtime/tmp/…/tasks/*.output`, which is inside the repository. The gate counts that file as a change outside the concept's files. My second run, with nothing changed, failed the same way on a new file, so more runs would only have used up budget. I stopped at 2 of 6.\n\n**What I wrote:**\n- **Concept body:** `kin_speed a > speed_tol ∧ |vx·dx + vy·dy| ≤ cos_max · kin_speed a`.\n  - `vx, vy` are the components of `kin_velocity a`, and `dx, dy` those of `st_lane_direction r`, which Core reads already normalised.\n  - The absolute value makes either crossing direction count.\n  - The speed test is strict, since moving at exactly `speed_tol` is excluded.\n  - There's no division; the bound is written as a product, the way the contract states it.\n  - It uses all three claimed atoms and only the formals `a` and `r`. I declared no narrowings because the contract fixes the comparison exactly.\n- **Witnesses:** lane 100 runs east (x 0–30 m, y −2 to 2 m). Pedestrian 1 starts at (10, 0) and walks at 1.4 m/s, over three scenes 1 s apart. The instance sets `speed_tol` to 0.1 m/s and `cos_max` to 1/2.\n  - **holds:** walking north, the dot product is 0, within 0.7.\n  - **fails:** walking east, it is 1.4, above 0.7.\n  - **blind:** walking north with `kin.velocity` hidden. The speed is still above the threshold, so the unknown velocity alone makes the result unknown.\n\nThe harness runs the gate again after this session, and that run should give the first real verdict. If it also reports `SCOPE_VIOLATION`, the fix is on the harness side: move the runtime's temporary folder out of the repository or have the gate ignore `.runtime/`. `task_result.md` records the two runs and this reasoning.",
